WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:02.002
♪ (SOMBER MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.339
(CROWS CAW)

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:12.345 line:0%
Easttown is a mixture
of a number of different towns

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:15.081 line:0%
in Pennsylvania
around where I grew up,

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.283
and I wanted the show
to be about

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:20.120
<i> a certain place in the world,
 a certain group of people.</i>

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.522
<i> These people
 have been born here.</i>

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:23.857
<i> They've been raised here,</i>

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:26.393
<i> and now they're raising
 their children here.</i>

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.496
KATE WINSLET:<i> That sense
 of history and shared history,</i>

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:33.432 line:0%
united history
that is very apparent

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:35.969 line:0%
in this particular area
of Easttown

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.971
is quite unique I would say,

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:40.239
and-- and everyone knows
a little bit

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.074
about what
someone else is up to,

00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:43.643
and there's always the sort of

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:46.245
<i> resident, slightly annoying
 character</i>

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:47.480
<i> Mare would have to deal with.</i>

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.549
SPEAKER:
Mare? Mare, you out there?

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:52.718
Oh, you're here.

00:00:52.818 --> 00:00:53.819
I'm here.

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:55.554
CRAIG ZOBEL:<i> We shot primarily</i>

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:58.091 line:0%
in what I would call
an exurb of Philadelphia,

00:00:58.191 --> 00:01:00.293 line:0%
kind of like
the southwestern area

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.995
<i> outside Chester County
 and Delaware County,</i>

00:01:03.095 --> 00:01:05.331
<i> and that really did inform
 so much.</i>

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.200
<i> It was nice to kind of be
 in a place</i>

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.069
<i> that felt like America,</i>

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:12.238
<i> and wasn't so specifically
 a big city.</i>

00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:15.174
<i> We wanted to make sure
 that the vibe of the show</i>

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:17.010
<i> and the feeling of Easttown</i>

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.944
<i> was specific
 in many different ways.</i>

00:01:19.044 --> 00:01:22.281
We did it through location.
We would try to pick a location

00:01:22.381 --> 00:01:24.584
that was true to what
it really was

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:26.586
<i> and not just
 the most picturesque.</i>

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.855
(HORN HONKS)

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:32.158
<i> We would do it with costume.</i>

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.594
Our costume designer
would constantly be texting me

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.929
pictures
from the Wawa convenience store,

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:38.697
<i> just of people in the line.</i>

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.068
JULIANNE NICHOLSON:
<i> The clothes really all felt
 lived in to me</i>

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.270
<i> and of that place.
 For instance,</i>

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.506 line:0%
I mean, I had, I think,
a Dave Matthews concert T-shirt

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:50.810 line:0%
and certain things that maybe
you won't even see on camera,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.444
but those little details
for her

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:55.181
<i> and the actors are important.</i>

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:58.785
I wanted the show to have
a grittiness to it

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.920
and have an honesty to it,
to have a realness to it.

00:02:02.821 --> 00:02:04.490
<i> These are people that get up
 every morning</i>

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.159
<i> and go to jobs that don't
 necessarily love,</i>

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:09.929
<i> but yeah, they do it
 out of a sense of duty.</i>

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.899
<i> So, I wanted to portray
 these communities that exist</i>

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.735
that don't often get any time
on the screen in a way

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.804
that was heroic and noble
and also really sympathetic.

00:02:18.904 --> 00:02:20.806
MARE SHEEHAN: We're asking
parents if their child

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:23.409
might have seen something'.
Please come forward.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.546
Every bit of information
helps at this point.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.382
GUY PEARCE:<i> What's beautiful
 about this story</i>

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:30.717 line:0%
is that it isn't
just about the crime,

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.851 line:0%
it isn't just about the mystery
and who's done it,

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:34.553
<i> but it's about the families.</i>

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.156
<i> You have the opportunity
 to sort of investigate</i>

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:39.092
<i> all the families
 and see the effects</i>

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:40.826
<i> that the crime has had
 and get involved</i>

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.930
<i>in everybody's personal stories
and see how they're interwoven.</i>

00:02:45.364 --> 00:02:48.067
So, there's something
kind of heightened, you know,

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.703
in the drama, I suppose,
when it is a small town.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:52.772
You're pushin' everyone away.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:56.742
Including you?

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:58.511
No, I won't let you.

00:03:00.078 --> 00:03:03.649
WINSLET:<i> The show is very much
 about compassion and mercy</i>

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:06.685
<i>and looking out for your friends
 and your neighbors,</i>

00:03:06.785 --> 00:03:08.855
and it's quite special
in that regard,

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.557
very moving in that regard.

